WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:02.539
1 Which event do you want to date?.

00:00:02.842 --> 00:00:05.829
2 What property of the sample will you measure?.

00:00:06.132 --> 00:00:09.882
3 Which method fits, and what does its date represent?.

00:00:10.184 --> 00:00:13.118
4 What additional information is needed?.

00:00:13.421 --> 00:00:18.316
Pause the video and pair the event of interest with a sample, a method and a statement

00:00:18.329 --> 00:00:21.552
explaining the meaning of the resulting age.

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:24.566
A. Charred seeds from a hearth.

00:00:25.028 --> 00:00:27.614
Date the activity involving fire.

00:00:28.068 --> 00:00:28.423
B.

00:00:28.489 --> 00:00:30.851
A timber with its outer wood removed.

00:00:31.305 --> 00:00:33.607
Date construction or repair.

00:00:34.068 --> 00:00:34.555
C.

00:00:34.607 --> 00:00:38.140
Fired material in an apparently undisturbed kiln.

00:00:38.594 --> 00:00:44.074
Use the examples on the slide to assess suitable material and its relation to the event.

00:00:44.528 --> 00:00:48.412
Preservation can determine which methods are possible.

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:51.367
Match the sample and clock.

00:00:51.823 --> 00:00:57.165
Which evidence fits: carbon, rings, magnetism or mineral signals?.

00:00:57.467 --> 00:00:59.632
Identify the dated event.

00:01:00.086 --> 00:01:04.994
Does the date concern formation, cooling, burial, felling or use?.

00:01:05.296 --> 00:01:07.448
Specify further checks.

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:13.658
Consider contamination, missing outer rings, movement, resetting and context.

00:01:14.112 --> 00:01:16.145
Give more than the method's name.

00:01:16.599 --> 00:01:20.809
Explain why the material is suitable and state what the resulting date would leave

00:01:20.823 --> 00:01:22.568
unresolved.

00:01:23.371 --> 00:01:25.082
A. Radiocarbon.

00:01:25.544 --> 00:01:27.307
Dates seed carbon.

00:01:27.768 --> 00:01:30.557
Assess its association with hearth use.

00:01:31.018 --> 00:01:31.413
B.

00:01:31.492 --> 00:01:33.123
Dendrochronology.

00:01:33.584 --> 00:01:35.577
Dates surviving rings.

00:01:36.031 --> 00:01:38.696
Assess missing outer wood and reuse.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:39.656
C.

00:01:39.715 --> 00:01:42.472
Archaeomagnetism, for example.

00:01:42.926 --> 00:01:47.689
Several methods may be applicable to a sample but date different processes.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:53.436
Compare them as distinct pieces of evidence rather than interchangeable numbers.

00:01:54.239 --> 00:01:57.054
1000 ± 30 BP.

00:01:57.186 --> 00:02:01.950
Example conventional radiocarbon age, referenced to 1950.

00:02:02.412 --> 00:02:06.235
Do not convert it to a calendar year by subtraction alone.

00:02:06.689 --> 00:02:10.439
cal BP / cal BCE / cal CE.

00:02:10.899 --> 00:02:13.208
Calibrated calendar ages.

00:02:13.662 --> 00:02:15.998
Record the curve and assumptions.

00:02:16.452 --> 00:02:20.399
cal BP also refers to years before 1950.

00:02:20.860 --> 00:02:23.360
1300 CE.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:26.432
Example of a ring’s calendar year.

00:02:26.886 --> 00:02:31.972
Without a reference convention and units, the same number can mean different things.

00:02:32.425 --> 00:02:37.072
Specify whether a result is a measured age or a calendar date.

00:02:37.875 --> 00:02:40.685
Choose methods for the sample and question.

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:44.733
Different samples from one site can date different events.

00:02:45.193 --> 00:02:48.575
Separate dating from environmental interpretation.

00:02:49.035 --> 00:02:53.436
Establish when a record formed, then investigate what it records.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:56.759
State the conditions of an interpretation.

00:02:57.219 --> 00:03:01.101
Combine dates, context and independent evidence.

00:03:01.561 --> 00:03:06.825
Keep track of the evidence supporting the date, environmental interpretation and causal

00:03:06.838 --> 00:03:08.180
explanation.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.476
These are related but separate steps in the argument.

00:03:13.279 --> 00:03:16.181
Finding reliable information in English.

00:03:16.637 --> 00:03:21.808
Use the English equivalents of Japanese terms as search words to find original research and

00:03:21.821 --> 00:03:24.480
datasets from other regions.

00:03:25.283 --> 00:03:27.547
Further reading and open data.

00:03:28.009 --> 00:03:31.101
Use the linked resources as starting points.

00:03:31.562 --> 00:03:37.660
When relying on a research finding, follow the route to its primary report and dataset.

00:03:38.463 --> 00:03:41.254
What does the vascular cambium produce?.

00:03:41.557 --> 00:03:44.158
How do earlywood and latewood differ?.

00:03:44.465 --> 00:03:47.728
Which features identify an annual boundary?.

00:03:48.031 --> 00:03:52.676
The next lesson examines where wood is produced and how a year's growth remains as a

00:03:52.689 --> 00:03:55.496
recognizable tissue increment.
